    <grid-item colspan="38" vertical-align="center">
      <h2>A philosophy of thought, self, and social life</h2>
      <p>Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel (1770–1831) asks how thought, nature, selfhood, and social life belong to a developing whole.</p>
      <grid direction="vertical" divider="sm neutral-border" vertical-sizing="hug">
        <grid-item padding="10pt 0">
          <h4>Logic</h4>
          <p>How categories shape what can be thought and made intelligible.</p>
        </grid-item>
        <grid-item padding="10pt 0">
          <h4>Spirit</h4>
          <p>Minded life as both individual and shared.</p>
        </grid-item>
        <grid-item padding="10pt 0">
          <h4>History</h4>
          <p>Changing social forms through which freedom is understood and organized.</p>
        </grid-item>
      </grid>
      <p font-size="sm">Source: <a href="https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/hegel/">Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Hegel”</a></p>
